\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Process Reward Models and Process Supervision.}
PRMs assign step-level quality scores \citep{xi2025agentprm,choudhury2025prm,fan2026agentrm}, building on process supervision for reasoning \citep{lightman2024letsverify,uesato2022processfeedback}, with benchmarks revealing long-horizon degradation \citep{wang2026planrewardbench,li2026toolprmbench}.
We differ in operating at window level without outcome labels, targeting real-time monitoring ($<$5\,ms) rather than training signal generation.

\paragraph{Agent Self-Reflection.}
Self-reflection methods \citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tot,zhou2024lats} prompt agents to diagnose failures, but LLMs cannot reliably self-correct without external feedback \citep{huang2024selfcorrect,kamoi2024selfcorrection}.
We address the prior question---\emph{when} is the agent stuck---using external structural signals.

\paragraph{Weak Supervision and Data-Centric AI.}
Data programming \citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel} enables dataset creation through labeling functions, extended via multi-task aggregation \citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal}, triplet methods \citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, sequential HMMs \citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, multi-resolution LFs \citep{sala2019multiresolution}, heterogeneous aggregation \citep{lison2021skweak}, and benchmarked systematically by WRENCH \citep{zhang2021wrench}.
Unlike \citeauthor{safranchik2020linkedhmm}'s NER results, temporal label models do not improve over majority vote in our low-conflict 3-LF regime (\S\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).
Active learning \citep{settles2009active} offers a complementary path for gold set expansion.

\paragraph{Noisy Labels and Label Leakage.}
Our silver labels connect to noisy label learning \citep{han2018coteaching,li2020dividemix}, though our noise is temporally block-correlated.
The structural feature--label overlap instantiates label leakage \citep{kaufman2012leakage} and Goodhart's Law \citep{manheim2018goodhart}; we mitigate this through cross-coding-agent evaluation and a human-annotated gold set (\S\ref{sec:gold_set}).

\paragraph{Agent Benchmarks and Monitoring.}
Agent evaluation spans diverse environments---general tasks \citep{liu2024agentbench}, web navigation \citep{zhou2024webarena}, interactive coding \citep{yang2024intercode}, tool use \citep{qin2024toolllm}, and debugging \citep{tian2024debugbench}---creating demand for scalable monitoring.
\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \citet{chen2025monitoring} establish that sequence-level context is necessary; \citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; \citet{ma2024agentboard} introduce fine-grained progress tracking; and \citet{cemri2025multiagentfail,zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes.
Runtime guardrail frameworks \citep{rebedea2023nemo,xiang2024guardagent} address safety but not progress detection.

\paragraph{Sequential Anomaly Detection, Process Mining, and Runtime Verification.}
Stagnation detection relates to sequential anomaly detection \citep{chandola2009anomaly}, including deep log analysis \citep{du2017deeplog,meng2019loganomaly} and runtime verification \citep{leucker2009runtime}.
Cycle detectors \citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.
Our structural features draw on process mining \citep{vanderaalst2016processmining}; the success of hand-crafted features parallels classical findings for narrow detection tasks \citep{liu2008isolationforest}.

\paragraph{Compute-Efficient Inference.}
Early-exit architectures \citep{teerapittayanon2016branchynet,schwartz2020righttool} and curriculum learning \citep{bengio2009curriculum} reduce computation by adapting effort to instance difficulty---analogous to stagnation detection, where the goal is terminating unproductive agent computation.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Process Supervision and Self-Reflection.}
PRMs assign step-level quality scores \citep{xi2025agentprm,choudhury2025prm,fan2026agentrm}, building on process supervision \citep{lightman2024letsverify,uesato2022processfeedback}, with benchmarks revealing long-horizon degradation \citep{wang2026planrewardbench,li2026toolprmbench}.
Self-reflection methods \citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tot,zhou2024lats} prompt agents to diagnose failures, but LLMs cannot reliably self-correct without external feedback \citep{huang2024selfcorrect,kamoi2024selfcorrection}.
We address a complementary question---\emph{when} is the agent stuck---using external structural signals at window level, targeting real-time monitoring ($<$5\,ms) without outcome labels.

\paragraph{Weak Supervision, Noisy Labels, and Label Leakage.}
Data programming \citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel} enables dataset creation through labeling functions, extended via multi-task aggregation \citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal}, triplet methods \citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, sequential HMMs \citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, multi-resolution LFs \citep{sala2019multiresolution}, and benchmarked by WRENCH \citep{zhang2021wrench}.
Unlike \citeauthor{safranchik2020linkedhmm}'s NER results, temporal label models do not improve over majority vote in our low-conflict 3-LF regime (\S\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).
Our silver labels connect to noisy label learning \citep{han2018coteaching,li2020dividemix}; the structural feature--label overlap instantiates label leakage \citep{kaufman2012leakage} and Goodhart's Law \citep{manheim2018goodhart}, mitigated through cross-coding-agent evaluation and a gold set (\S\ref{sec:gold_set}).
Active learning \citep{settles2009active} offers a complementary path for gold set expansion.

\paragraph{Agent Benchmarks and Monitoring.}
Agent evaluation spans diverse environments---general tasks \citep{liu2024agentbench}, web navigation \citep{zhou2024webarena}, interactive coding \citep{yang2024intercode}, tool use \citep{qin2024toolllm}, and debugging \citep{tian2024debugbench}---creating demand for scalable monitoring.
\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \citet{chen2025monitoring} establish that sequence-level context is necessary; \citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \citet{cemri2025multiagentfail,zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes.
Runtime guardrail frameworks \citep{rebedea2023nemo,xiang2024guardagent} address safety but not progress detection.

\paragraph{Anomaly Detection, Process Mining, and Compute Efficiency.}
Stagnation detection relates to sequential anomaly detection \citep{chandola2009anomaly}, including deep log analysis \citep{du2017deeplog,meng2019loganomaly} and runtime verification \citep{leucker2009runtime}.
Cycle detectors \citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.
Our structural features draw on process mining \citep{vanderaalst2016processmining}; the success of hand-crafted features parallels classical findings for narrow detection tasks \citep{liu2008isolationforest}.
Early-exit architectures \citep{teerapittayanon2016branchynet,schwartz2020righttool} and curriculum learning \citep{bengio2009curriculum} reduce computation by adapting effort to instance difficulty---analogous to stagnation detection, where the goal is terminating unproductive agent computation.

